<spell-notebook "root usage">
	<header level="1">
		Root
		<header>A root loads the components inside it on demand, sets their theme, size, icons and emoji, and shows
              them once they're ready.</>
	</>
	<root-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#loading">Loading</>
			<item link="#component-packs">Component packs</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  from the old Astro page's Behaviour and Accessibility, plus the vocabulary's events. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Load the root, put one around the page (or each app on it), and write `ui-*` tags anywhere inside: no other
			import, no registration.

			- `display`, `loading` and `timeout` say what shows while components load (see While loading, on the Examples
			  tab).
			- `theme`, `size`, `icons` and `emoji` apply to everything inside; a nested root inherits what it doesn't set.
			- `width` / `height` / `fixed` make it a box that scrolls its own content; name each box with `aria-label`.
			- `assets` is the folder the built-in icon packs load from, relative to the page (default: beside the library).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- once, in your entry:  import "spell/ui/ui-root" -->
			<ui-root icons="fa7-brands" loading="Loading the dashboard" fixed aria-label="Dashboard">
			  <ui-menu>...</ui-menu>
			  <ui-grid>...</ui-grid>
			</ui-root>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Loading">
		<markdown>
			- Each undefined `ui-*` tag inside (now, and whenever content is added) imports its component once per page.
			  Which component defines a tag comes from a generated catalog (`yarn gen:root` after adding a tag).
			- Ready: every component imported, then every element inside ready (an inner root: settled). Then
			  `:state(ready)`, `ui-ready` with `{ failed }`, and the content shows. Content added later loads too, but is
			  never hidden again.
			- Failures: a tag no component defines (`unknown`), a component that can't be imported (`failed`), or one not
			  ready by `timeout` (`timeout`) fires a cancelable `ui-error` (cancel it to skip the console warning) and is
			  listed in `ui-ready`'s `failed`. The content shows anyway;
			  a broken form control shows its native fallback, any other broken element just its children.
			- Without JavaScript (or before the root's script runs, with `spell/ui/styles` loaded), an undefined root
			  hides its content only under `@media (scripting: enabled)`: server-rendered pages still show as sent.
			- Swap the loading look in code: `UIRoot.Loading` (a `<ui-loader>`) and `UIRoot.Skeleton` (`<ui-placeholder>`s).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Component packs">
		<markdown>
			A component pack is another package's elements (any tag prefix: `epic-`, `x-` ...), loaded on demand like
			Spell UI's own. Name it with a `<ui-components source>` anywhere inside the root; it draws nothing.

			- The root loads each distinct `source` once per page, as a classic `<script>` (relative to the page, so it
			  works from `file://` too), and waits for it before it's ready.
			- The pack's script calls `SpellUI.registerPack({ name, prefix, catalog, define })` as it runs: `define()`
			  defines its tags, and `catalog` describes each one as Spell UI's own catalog does (its skeleton). It shares
			  the page's Solid and Spell UI through `SpellUI.packModules`, never a copy of its own.
			- Then the root treats the pack's tags as its own: ready waits for them, `display="skeleton"` draws their
			  skeletons, and a tag with the pack's prefix that it doesn't define is reported (`unknown`).
			- A pack that can't load, or doesn't register, still lets the root get ready: `ui-error` and `ui-ready`'s
			  `failed` list it as `{ tag: "ui-components", reason, source }`, and the console shows an error naming its
			  `source`.
			- In an ES module bundle, `import { registerPack } from "spell/ui"` (or `spell/ui/ui-components`).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-root>
			  <ui-components source="packs/epics.pack.js"></ui-components>
			  <epic-page epic="seo" title="SEO">...</epic-page>
			</ui-root>
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			// epics.pack.js, as `spell pack build` writes it (a classic script)
			SpellUI.registerPack({
			  name: "epics",
			  prefix: "epic-",
			  catalog: { "epic-page": { folder: "epic-page", skeleton: { parts: [{ shape: "header" }] } } },
			  define() {
			    customElements.define("epic-page", EpicPage)
			  }
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-ready` -- everything inside is ready (or the timeout passed) and the content shows: `{ failed }`, each
			  `{ tag, reason, error }`.
			- `ui-error` (cancelable) -- a tag inside couldn't load: `{ tag, reason, error }`, `reason` one of `unknown`,
			  `failed`, `timeout`; or a component pack didn't: `tag` is `ui-components`, with its `source`. Cancel it
			  to skip the console warning.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const root = document.querySelector("ui-root")
			root.addEventListener("ui-error", (event) => {
			  if (event.detail.tag === "ui-calendar") event.preventDefault() // optional:  no warning
			})
			root.addEventListener("ui-ready", (event) => console.log("ready;  failed:", event.detail.failed))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Nothing of its own. A root with a box (`width`, `height` or `fixed`) is one tab stop, so the arrow keys and
			**Page Up** / **Page Down** can scroll it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- While loading, the content is hidden (`visibility: hidden` or not drawn), so assistive technology doesn't read
			  half-built UI. The placeholders are decorative (`aria-hidden`).
			- The `loading` message is a `<ui-loader>`: `role="status"`, announced politely.
			- Without a box (no `width` / `height` / `fixed`) the root adds nothing to the page's structure: headings and
			  landmarks inside are the page's own.
			- With a box, the scroller is a region named by the host's `aria-label` (else "Content"): name each one when a
			  page has several.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
